 "overview": {
  "summary": "The output stage of the Wideband AC Module, and the reason a 5700A can source a calibrated sine wave to 30 MHz. It takes a sine wave from one of two places — the Oscillator Output assembly (A13) below 1.1 MHz, or the Wideband Oscillator assembly (A6) above 1.2 MHz — and amplifies it to drive 3.5 V rms into a 50 Ω load at the front panel type N connector. Three things sit around that amplifier and matter as much as it does. A thermal rms sensor (U1) measures the output as a dc voltage, which is what makes the amplitude accurate rather than merely large; a 50 Ω resistive attenuator built from two networks (Z1, Z2) and five relays (K4–K8) reduces it in 10 dB steps from 0 to 70 dB, giving a continuous range of 300 µV to 3.5 V rms; and an overload circuit shunts the sensor and drops out every attenuator relay if the sensor output goes 10% past full scale. Digital control is an 82C55 (U7) driving three relay drivers and a diagnostic multiplexer over the guarded bus. Which of the three operating ranges the board is in decides where the amplitude control comes from: below 12 kHz A12 controls it and A5's loop only protects; from 12 kHz to 1.1 MHz A5's sensor output goes to A11's adc through the RCL line; above 1.2 MHz A5 compares its sensor against DAC OUT and drives A6's amplitude control line directly.",
  "sections": "§2-222 … §2-228",
  "note": "A5 is part of Option -03 and is fitted only to instruments that carry the wideband option. On a calibrator without it, the A5 and A6 card slots are empty and Table 6-6 describes a board that is not in the instrument — so if this assembly cannot be found, check the option before suspecting it has been removed. Fault code 3904 is the instrument's own way of saying the same thing. A5 generates no supply of its own: +17 S, S COM and −17 S arrive on P111 19A/19C, 21A/21C and 20A/20C, +5 LH, LH COM and −5 LH on 13A/13C, 15A/15C and 17A/17C, and +5 RLH with RLH COM on 14A/14C and 16A/16C. Six 6-turn chokes (L1–L6) with the tantalums beside them turn each incoming rail into its own S-suffixed net — +17 S becomes +17S, +5 RLH becomes +SRLH — so a fault that looks like one section misbehaving may be one of those six chokes. The board's two coaxial connectors are not interchangeable: J1 is the 1.2–30 MHz input from A6, J2 the output to the front panel."
 },
